---
import { docsHref, docsSections } from "@intentic-dev/site-content/docs";
import DocsLayout from "../../components/DocsLayout.astro";

/* The overview's job is to route, not to summarise. Two questions bring people here — "what IS this?" and
 * "how do I get one?" — and the second used to be answered by a grid of ten cards that made Quickstart a
 * peer of Manifest reference. So the install decision is now stated as the four routes it actually is, with
 * links straight into the section of Quickstart that answers each, and the card grid drops to the sections
 * that are genuinely further reading (everything outside "Get started"). */

const readingSections = docsSections.filter((section) => section.label !== `Get started`);

// The install decision, in the order a reader eliminates them: least typing first.
const paths = [
    {
        name: `The desktop app`,
        href: `/docs/quickstart/#desktop-app`,
        who: `You'd rather not open a terminal, and want updates as a button.`,
        run: `An installer, then one click`,
    },
    {
        name: `The setup command`,
        href: `/docs/quickstart/#setup-command`,
        who: `You're on a server, a Mac, or anywhere over SSH.`,
        run: `One pasted line`,
    },
    {
        name: `Docker Compose`,
        href: `/docs/quickstart/#docker-compose`,
        who: `You want to read the whole definition before anything runs.`,
        run: `docker compose up -d`,
    },
    {
        name: `Plain docker run`,
        href: `/docs/quickstart/#docker-run`,
        who: `You're scripting this into your own provisioning tooling.`,
        run: `Five docker commands`,
    },
];
---

<DocsLayout
    pageId=""
    heading="intentic documentation"
    lead="intentic gives each coding agent its own sandbox: a specialized workspace on hardware you own. These docs cover getting one running, how the system fits together, and how to turn a sandbox into an autonomous employee."
>
    <h2>The mental model</h2>
    <p>
        One idea holds the whole product together: <strong>a specialized agent is a purpose-built sandbox.</strong> Not a prompt or a
        markdown file. It is a real container, on a machine you control, with the libraries, dev-tools, system access, and curated context a
        specific job needs. You drive it from any browser; it runs where you started it.
    </p>
    <p>Two tiers make that work, and only your sandbox holds your code:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>The platform</strong>: a thin hub that stores your identity and your sandbox's URL. It sits off the command path and can't reach in.</li>
        <li>
            <strong>Your sandbox</strong>: one container per agent, on your hardware, reached by your browser over a private Cloudflare
            tunnel. The agent, repos, credentials, and capabilities live here, along with any tool it runs: a database client, a headless
            browser, even a deployment engine.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Read <a href="/docs/architecture/">Architecture</a> for the full picture, or bring one up now.
    </p>

    <h2>Get one running</h2>
    <p>
        Every route starts the same way — sign in at the platform, which mints a short-lived setup code — and every route ends with the same
        two containers on your machine. They differ only in who types the docker commands.
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Route</th>
                <th>Choose it when</th>
                <th>You run</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {
                paths.map((path) => (
                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            <a href={path.href}>{path.name}</a>
                        </td>
                        <td>{path.who}</td>
                        <td>{path.run}</td>
                    </tr>
                ))
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
        <a href="/docs/quickstart/">Quickstart</a> walks all four end to end with copy-pasteable commands.
        <a href="/docs/docker/">Docker setup</a> is the anatomy they share: what gets created, where your data lives, and how to update or
        remove it. If you'd like the installer without reading further, it's on the <a href="/download/">download page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What's in a sandbox</h2>
    <p>Everything an agent needs to do real work, and nothing the platform can see:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>An agent</strong>: Claude Code, Codex, or Grok, on your own subscription, chosen per conversation.</li>
        <li><strong>A workspace</strong>: your repos, a file tree, editors, and terminals that survive reconnects.</li>
        <li><strong>Capabilities</strong>: GitHub, databases, Sentry, Stripe, SSH hosts, MCP servers, added in a click.</li>
        <li><strong>An environment</strong>: libraries and dev-tools baked into the image, proposed by the agent and applied on your approval.</li>
        <li><strong>Automations</strong>: schedules, webhooks, and event listeners that wake the agent while you're away.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Once it's up</h2>
    {
        readingSections.map((section) => (
            <div class="docs-embed mt-6">
                <div class="eyebrow mb-3">{section.label}</div>
                <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
                    {section.items.map((page) => (
                        <a href={docsHref(page.id)} class="card p-5 no-underline transition-colors hover:border-primary-600/40">
                            <h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-content">{page.title}</h3>
                            <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">{page.meta.description}</p>
                        </a>
                    ))}
                </div>
            </div>
        ))
    }
</DocsLayout>
